WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.349
The next optimization view mode I'd like to talk about

2
00:00:04.349 --> 00:00:05.969
is Nanite.

3
00:00:05.969 --> 00:00:11.349
Now, Nanite has a number of different optimization view

4
00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:15.070
modes we can see to inspect everything that's going on.

5
00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.429
And the reason that I want to bring this up

6
00:00:17.429 --> 00:00:20.829
is while all of these view modes that Nanite offers

7
00:00:20.829 --> 00:00:24.429
are useful, it is the view mode

8
00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:27.070
that is happening on this slide that

9
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.550
is of most importance to us.

10
00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:32.729
The Nanite overdraw view mode.

11
00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:35.649
The overdraw view mode here will show us

12
00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:38.329
areas which Nanite meshes have been

13
00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:40.450
stacked over the top of one another

14
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.929
and could present performance issues.

15
00:00:42.929 --> 00:00:46.009
While Nanite is very, very, very powerful,

16
00:00:46.009 --> 00:00:49.530
there are certain things that we need to be aware of in

17
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.429
order to make sure that we wield this power responsibly.

18
00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:57.149
And one of those is that stacking multiple Nanite meshes

19
00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.060
on the top of one another can make Nanite

20
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.299
perform less than optimal.

21
00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:06.219
And the Nanite overdraw mode will show us this.

22
00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:08.319
And this mode, as with our other ones,

23
00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:11.439
we're looking for areas of very bright orange.

24
00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:13.459
That's going to show us areas

25
00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:16.480
where it could be very expensive to render

26
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.579
the Nanite in that particular area.

27
00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:21.400
Again, like with all of our optimization view modes,

28
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.980
what we're trying to do is make sure

29
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.310
that we have a general ideal situation going on

30
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.750
across our entire map, and that the areas that

31
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.189
are very expensive or expensive are worth that cost.

32
00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:37.469
If it's happening in an area that's, say,

33
00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:40.109
out of the main area of interaction,

34
00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:42.510
then we're going to want to optimize that area.

35
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.670
However, if we do have some expensive and very expensive

36
00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.069
areas, but they're within the area the user is going

37
00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:52.909
to interact with, they might be worth that expense

38
00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:56.340
depending on the setup you have in fall.

39
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.540
Again, remember, the idea here is to mitigate

40
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.459
and not completely eliminate and make sure

41
00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:04.819
that you keep an eye on areas that could possibly

42
00:02:04.819 --> 00:02:07.980
have performance issues or areas that are suffering

43
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.060
from performance issues.

44
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.819
And this gives you a good idea of why they are.
